1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:04,862
A dart player hits the same spot
every time, but far from the bullseye.

2
00:00:04,862 --> 00:00:06,398
Is that a good player?

3
00:00:06,798 --> 00:00:10,022
In physics, the answer depends
on two different words.

4
00:00:10,472 --> 00:00:14,617
Diya weighs the same packet
of dal on two kitchen scales.

5
00:00:14,617 --> 00:00:17,615
The packet is exactly five hundred grams.

6
00:00:17,965 --> 00:00:22,513
Scale one reads five hundred and
twelve, five hundred and eleven, five

7
00:00:22,513 --> 00:00:23,918
hundred and twelve.

8
00:00:23,918 --> 00:00:25,434
The same every time.

9
00:00:25,434 --> 00:00:27,886
Scale two jumps around five hundred.

10
00:00:28,386 --> 00:00:29,779
Which scale is better?

11
00:00:29,779 --> 00:00:33,536
By the end of this video, you
will be able to say exactly why.

12
00:00:34,186 --> 00:00:36,135
Two words, two different ideas.

13
00:00:36,485 --> 00:00:40,312
Accuracy is how close a
measurement is to the true value.

14
00:00:40,712 --> 00:00:44,375
Precision is how close repeated
measurements are to each other.

15
00:00:44,775 --> 00:00:47,267
Accuracy asks: are we near the truth?

16
00:00:47,267 --> 00:00:50,044
Precision asks: do we
agree with ourselves?

17
00:00:50,694 --> 00:00:53,687
Let's test both scales
on our measuring bench.

18
00:00:53,687 --> 00:00:55,073
Five readings each.

19
00:00:55,423 --> 00:00:56,323
Scale one.

20
00:00:56,274 --> 00:01:00,938
Five hundred and twelve, five hundred
and eleven, five hundred and twelve,

21
00:01:00,938 --> 00:01:04,243
five hundred and thirteen,
five hundred and twelve.

22
00:01:04,643 --> 00:01:06,825
The dots sit in a tight bunch.

23
00:01:06,825 --> 00:01:08,896
The spread is only two grams.

24
00:01:08,896 --> 00:01:09,921
Very precise.

25
00:01:10,371 --> 00:01:13,744
But the bunch sits twelve
grams above the true value.

26
00:01:13,744 --> 00:01:14,727
Not accurate.

27
00:01:15,177 --> 00:01:16,077
Scale two.

28
00:01:15,999 --> 00:01:20,225
Four hundred and ninety four, five
hundred and seven, four hundred and

29
00:01:20,225 --> 00:01:24,440
ninety seven, five hundred and
five, four hundred and ninety seven.

30
00:01:24,840 --> 00:01:27,679
The dots spread out over thirteen grams.

31
00:01:27,679 --> 00:01:28,710
Not precise.

32
00:01:29,160 --> 00:01:32,002
But their average is exactly five hundred.

33
00:01:32,002 --> 00:01:33,622
On average, accurate.

34
00:01:34,272 --> 00:01:37,257
Let's turn this into
numbers, one step at a time.

35
00:01:37,607 --> 00:01:38,507
Step one.

36
00:01:38,409 --> 00:01:39,515
Take the mean.

37
00:01:39,515 --> 00:01:42,792
Add the readings and divide
by how many there are.

38
00:01:43,542 --> 00:01:44,442
Step two.

39
00:01:44,406 --> 00:01:45,306
Accuracy.

40
00:01:45,207 --> 00:01:47,914
Subtract the true value from the mean.

41
00:01:47,914 --> 00:01:49,916
A small gap means accurate.

42
00:01:50,666 --> 00:01:51,627
Step three.

43
00:01:51,627 --> 00:01:52,527
Precision.

44
00:01:52,472 --> 00:01:56,326
Find the spread, the largest
reading minus the smallest.

45
00:01:56,326 --> 00:01:58,414
A small spread means precise.

46
00:01:59,164 --> 00:02:00,238
Remember this.

47
00:02:00,238 --> 00:02:02,607
Accuracy is closeness to the truth.

48
00:02:02,607 --> 00:02:05,076
Precision is closeness to each other.

49
00:02:05,776 --> 00:02:06,819
Side by side.

50
00:02:06,819 --> 00:02:10,661
Scale one has a gap of twelve
grams, and a spread of two.

51
00:02:11,061 --> 00:02:14,397
Scale two has a gap of zero,
and a spread of thirteen.

52
00:02:14,997 --> 00:02:16,032
Now you try.

53
00:02:16,032 --> 00:02:19,348
Scale one is wrong by the
same amount every time.

54
00:02:19,348 --> 00:02:21,518
Can a single correction fix it?

55
00:02:21,518 --> 00:02:22,804
Pause and decide.

56
00:02:26,004 --> 00:02:26,904
Yes!

57
00:02:26,511 --> 00:02:29,153
Subtract twelve grams from every reading.

58
00:02:29,153 --> 00:02:32,272
A steady error like this
is a systematic error.

59
00:02:32,272 --> 00:02:34,775
It shifts every reading the same way.

60
00:02:35,425 --> 00:02:38,495
Precision also depends
on the instrument itself.

61
00:02:38,845 --> 00:02:42,833
Say a length is truly three point
six seven eight centimetres.

62
00:02:43,233 --> 00:02:47,456
An instrument that reads to zero
point one centimetre gives three point

63
00:02:47,456 --> 00:02:48,356
five.

64
00:02:48,403 --> 00:02:52,886
A finer instrument reading to zero
point zero one, if badly set, could

65
00:02:52,886 --> 00:02:54,865
give three point three eight.

66
00:02:55,265 --> 00:02:59,671
The finer reading is more precise,
but three point five is closer to the

67
00:02:59,671 --> 00:03:01,792
truth, so it is more accurate.

68
00:03:01,792 --> 00:03:04,211
More digits do not mean more correct.

69
00:03:04,861 --> 00:03:07,008
Physicists picture this on a target.

70
00:03:07,358 --> 00:03:10,192
Tight bunch on the centre:
accurate and precise.

71
00:03:10,592 --> 00:03:13,572
Tight bunch, off centre:
precise but not accurate.

72
00:03:13,972 --> 00:03:18,185
Wide spread around the centre:
accurate on average, but not precise.

73
00:03:18,585 --> 00:03:20,526
Wide spread, off centre: neither.

74
00:03:21,176 --> 00:03:22,087
Your turn.

75
00:03:22,087 --> 00:03:25,587
A clock runs exactly two
minutes fast, every day.

76
00:03:25,587 --> 00:03:27,242
Precise, or accurate?

77
00:03:27,242 --> 00:03:28,583
Pause and decide.

78
00:03:31,783 --> 00:03:33,480
Precise, but not accurate!

79
00:03:33,480 --> 00:03:37,646
Its readings agree day after day, but
they are always two minutes off the

80
00:03:37,646 --> 00:03:38,546
true time.

81
00:03:39,077 --> 00:03:40,812
Now, one worked example.

82
00:03:41,162 --> 00:03:44,031
A rod is truly fifty
point zero centimetres.

83
00:03:44,031 --> 00:03:46,646
Two students measure it four times each.

84
00:03:46,646 --> 00:03:49,770
Who is more accurate,
and who is more precise?

85
00:03:50,270 --> 00:03:51,356
Means first.

86
00:03:51,356 --> 00:03:54,524
P's readings add to
two hundred point four.

87
00:03:54,524 --> 00:03:55,845
Divide by four.

88
00:03:55,845 --> 00:03:57,222
Fifty point one.

89
00:03:57,972 --> 00:04:01,631
Q's readings add to two
hundred and eight point eight.

90
00:04:01,631 --> 00:04:02,865
Divide by four.

91
00:04:02,865 --> 00:04:04,422
Fifty two point two.

92
00:04:05,172 --> 00:04:06,072
Gaps.

93
00:04:05,765 --> 00:04:08,716
P is zero point one away from the truth.

94
00:04:08,716 --> 00:04:10,631
Q is two point two away.

95
00:04:10,631 --> 00:04:12,376
So P is more accurate.

96
00:04:13,126 --> 00:04:14,026
Spreads.

97
00:04:13,896 --> 00:04:16,790
P's readings spread over zero point four.

98
00:04:16,790 --> 00:04:19,414
Q's spread over only zero point two.

99
00:04:19,414 --> 00:04:21,148
So Q is more precise.

100
00:04:21,798 --> 00:04:23,264
Now, the exam trap.

101
00:04:23,614 --> 00:04:27,465
Many students think that if readings
agree with each other, they must be

102
00:04:27,465 --> 00:04:28,365
correct.

103
00:04:28,468 --> 00:04:30,732
But agreement only shows precision.

104
00:04:30,732 --> 00:04:35,241
A zero error shifts every reading by
the same amount, so precise readings

105
00:04:35,241 --> 00:04:36,659
can still be wrong.

106
00:04:37,309 --> 00:04:38,792
Where is this asked?

107
00:04:38,792 --> 00:04:43,291
As concept questions comparing sets
of readings, and in vernier and screw

108
00:04:43,291 --> 00:04:44,555
gauge experiments.

109
00:04:45,155 --> 00:04:46,371
Exam level now.

110
00:04:46,371 --> 00:04:49,710
A true length is ten point
zero zero millimetres.

111
00:04:49,710 --> 00:04:53,101
Which set of readings is
precise but not accurate?

112
00:04:53,101 --> 00:04:54,369
Pause and solve.

113
00:04:59,769 --> 00:05:01,039
The answer is C.

114
00:05:01,539 --> 00:05:05,122
A is close to the truth but
spread over zero point four.

115
00:05:05,122 --> 00:05:06,842
B is spread out and off.

116
00:05:06,842 --> 00:05:08,658
D is both close and tight.

117
00:05:09,158 --> 00:05:13,963
C spreads over only zero point zero
two, but its mean is ten point four

118
00:05:13,963 --> 00:05:14,863
one.

119
00:05:14,495 --> 00:05:17,608
That is zero point four
one millimetres away.

120
00:05:17,608 --> 00:05:19,310
Precise, not accurate.

121
00:05:19,910 --> 00:05:21,369
Here is your recap.

122
00:05:21,369 --> 00:05:22,869
Screenshot this card.

123
00:05:26,069 --> 00:05:28,905
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

124
00:05:29,305 --> 00:05:30,205
One.

125
00:05:29,830 --> 00:05:33,359
What is the difference between
accuracy and precision?

126
00:05:34,259 --> 00:05:35,159
Two.

127
00:05:34,768 --> 00:05:38,111
Can a set of readings be
precise but not accurate?

128
00:05:38,111 --> 00:05:39,339
Give an example.

129
00:05:40,239 --> 00:05:41,139
Three.

130
00:05:40,883 --> 00:05:45,545
Why does an instrument with finer
markings give more precise readings?

131
00:05:46,645 --> 00:05:50,470
Next time: what is the smallest
length Aarav's ruler can read?

132
00:05:50,470 --> 00:05:53,664
We find out in least count
of measuring instruments.
